{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_29_1300642143#9_2945582694","relevance":0,"reason":"The passage is about The Body Shop's Drops of Youth product and a corporate anti-animal-testing campaign; 'Youth' is a product name, not teenagers.","learned":"Queries using 'youth' can retrieve irrelevant product-name matches."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ teenager teen student) \"animal testing\" (+ started launched organized created) (+ petition campaign protest))","reason":"Search for documents where a teenager or student is explicitly described as starting or organizing an anti-animal-testing action."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ teenager teen student) \"animal testing\" (+ started launched organized created) (+ petition campaign protest))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_302508461#8_556340766","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q987","rank":1,"snippet":"We take a look at some of the key milestones in the history of the Animal Liberation Front: The seeds of the Animal Liberation Front movement were sown back in 1963, when a British journalist was assigned to cover a stag hunt. After watching the hunters chase and kill a pregnant deer, he felt compelled to set up the Hunt Saboteurs Association (HSA) in <cover>protest of what he had seen. HSA groups started to spring up around the country, including one in Luton, formed by a law student called Ronnie Lee in 1972. Lee, and another activist, Cliff Goodman, felt that more militant direct action tactics were needed, so formed their own group, The Bands of Mercy. Between 1973 and 1974, the Bands of Mercy embarked on a series of attacks on several animal testing </cover>research laboratories before completing its first act of animal liberation, taking guinea pigs from a Wiltshire farm. In August 1974, Lee and Goodman went on trial for a raid on an Oxfordshire laboratory and given a prison sentence. On Lee’s release in 1976, he was more focused than ever and renamed the Bands of Mercy as the Animal Liberation Front. From the beginning, the "}}
