{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_555980527#10_982777753","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives student-focused actions against animal use in education, including talking to other students and school officials, creating a petition against dissection, getting parent letters, and contacting a dissection hotline. It is useful supporting material for student/teen action against animal experiments, but it is mainly advice about dissection rather than evidence of teens actually stopping animal testing.","learned":"Students can oppose animal use by organizing with classmates, petitioning against dissection at school, seeking parental support letters, and using dissection hotlines or student-choice policies."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1027","reason":"This query has produced a promising student-action result around dissection/vivisection alternatives; more results may contain direct examples of teen or student campaigns, petitions, or refusals."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1027"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_312882363#0_716443810","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1027","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_312882363#0_716443810\" , \"end_char\": 1248.000000 , \"headings\": \" Dissection Alternatives Video Dissections Virtual Dissections 3-D Scienstructable Dissection Models Synthetic Specimens Dissections Using Real Animal Specimens Related Posts \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Dissection Alternatives for High School </cover>Biology - Kristin Moon Science 2 Comments / Animals, Biology, High School, Science Experiments, Teaching helps What do you think of when you hear the word dissection? Does the word bring back memories of stinky formaldehyde and slimy frogs? Do you feel a bit queasy? If so, you’re not alone. Parents, teachers, and students are pretty divided when it comes to the topic of dissections. It seems as though folks either think dissections are great or they hate them. No matter how you feel about dissections, there’s no denying that they are a great tool for learning about animal anatomy. For one thing, hands-on activities are always more effective at making learning stick than studying material in a textbook. Dissections allow students to better understand animal anatomy and how all of the organ systems work together"}}
